1
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:11,714
Last time we asked whether every set of lists has a stable matching.

2
00:00:11,714 --> 00:00:15,576
Before we answer that, look at a close cousin of the problem.

3
00:00:15,576 --> 00:00:21,380
Four students, Amy, Ben, Cora and Dan, have to pair up as roommates.

4
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:26,508
This time there are no two sides, so anybody can end up with anybody.

5
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:29,392
Each of them ranks the other three.

6
00:00:29,392 --> 00:00:34,889
Amy would like Ben best and then Cora, Ben would like Cora best and then Amy,

7
00:00:34,889 --> 00:00:37,888
and Cora would like Amy best and then Ben.

8
00:00:37,888 --> 00:00:43,190
All three put Dan last, and we leave the list of Dan open for now.

9
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:49,186
Let us start with any matching, say Amy with Ben and Cora with Dan.

10
00:00:49,186 --> 00:00:51,934
Ben has Amy, but his first choice is Cora,

11
00:00:51,934 --> 00:00:56,450
and Cora is stuck with her last choice, so she would gladly take Ben.

12
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:02,490
So Ben and Cora are a rogue couple, and this matching is unstable.

13
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:06,448
The obvious repair is to give the rogue couple what they want.

14
00:01:06,448 --> 00:01:13,026
So Ben moves in with Cora, and the two who are left behind, Amy and Dan, share the other room.

15
00:01:13,026 --> 00:01:18,902
Each repair gets rid of one rogue couple, so surely we run out of them in the end.

16
00:01:19,083 --> 00:01:24,725
Ben is content now, but look at Cora, who has Ben and still ranks Amy above him.

17
00:01:24,725 --> 00:01:29,687
And Amy has been pushed down to her last choice, so she would gladly take Cora.

18
00:01:29,687 --> 00:01:35,791
The repair removed one rogue couple and created a new one, Amy and Cora.

19
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:41,022
So we repair again, which puts Amy with Cora and leaves Ben with Dan.

20
00:01:41,022 --> 00:01:46,488
Now it is Ben who sits on his last choice, and Amy still ranks Ben above Cora.

21
00:01:46,488 --> 00:01:50,426
That makes Amy and Ben the next rogue couple.

22
00:01:50,583 --> 00:01:55,323
Repair once more, and Amy is with Ben and Cora is with Dan.

23
00:01:55,323 --> 00:01:58,373
But that is exactly the matching we started from.

24
00:01:58,373 --> 00:02:06,093
The repairs run in a circle and never finish, so this recipe does not always lead to a stable matching.

25
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:13,426
Maybe the recipe was just unlucky and a stable matching hides somewhere else.

26
00:02:13,426 --> 00:02:15,770
Let us count the possibilities.

27
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:23,270
Amy shares with Ben, with Cora, or with Dan, and each choice leaves the other two no option but each other.

28
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:29,300
So there are only three matchings, and we have just seen a rogue couple in every one of them.

29
00:02:29,483 --> 00:02:31,745
There is a pattern behind this.

30
00:02:31,745 --> 00:02:37,511
Whoever shares with Dan has landed on their last choice and would rather be with anyone else.

31
00:02:37,511 --> 00:02:43,571
Now look at the first choices, where Amy wants Ben, Ben wants Cora, and Cora wants Amy.

32
00:02:43,571 --> 00:02:48,439
So whoever is with Dan is always somebody's first choice.

33
00:02:48,616 --> 00:02:53,122
That somebody cannot be with their first choice, because Dan has taken it.

34
00:02:53,122 --> 00:02:56,487
So the two of them would both rather have each other,

35
00:02:56,487 --> 00:03:00,232
and they form a rogue couple whatever the list of Dan says.

36
00:03:02,383 --> 00:03:06,275
So for roommates a stable matching does not have to exist.

37
00:03:06,275 --> 00:03:11,689
Now remember the repair recipe, which never asked who was a job and who was a candidate.

38
00:03:11,689 --> 00:03:16,197
If it proved that jobs and candidates always have a stable matching,

39
00:03:16,197 --> 00:03:20,373
the same words would prove it for roommates, and that is false.

40
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:25,424
So any proof for jobs and candidates has to use the two sides somewhere.

41
00:03:25,424 --> 00:03:32,396
Propose and reject does exactly that, because only jobs make offers and only candidates answer.

42
00:03:32,396 --> 00:03:37,862
Whether it always ends in a stable matching is what the next episodes work out.

